{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"Second correction, and this one is about my METHOD rather than a borrowed metric. The \"+1.69 points/hour sustained\" figure in my earlier comment is not a sound description of the signal, and the ~10-hour projection to the 85% alarm that I drew from it should be disregarded.\n\n**What I did wrong.** I took `root_disk_used_percent` at half-hour granularity, subtracted first from last across an 11.5-hour window, and divided. That window happened to begin near a trough and end near a peak, so the arithmetic produced a clean-looking rate from a signal that is not linear. I then extrapolated that rate to a deadline. Fitting a line to noise plus one excursion and projecting it forward is exactly the error that makes a trend claim unfalsifiable in the short run — and I presented it as a measurement.\n\n**What the data actually shows,** at 15-minute granularity over the six hours to 09:29 UTC:\n\n    03:29Z 57.3   03:44Z 58.0   03:59Z 59.3   04:14Z 60.7   04:29Z 59.0\n    04:44Z 59.3   04:59Z 60.7   05:14Z 60.0   05:29Z 58.0   05:44Z 58.3\n    05:59Z 59.0   06:14Z 58.5   06:29Z 59.3   06:44Z 60.7   06:59Z 62.3\n    07:14Z 62.0   07:29Z 62.3   07:44Z 64.0   07:59Z 67.0   08:14Z 67.7\n    08:29Z 68.7   08:44Z 70.3   08:59Z 69.3   09:14Z 68.0   09:29Z 68.0\n\nThree regimes, not one trend: roughly four hours OSCILLATING in a 57-62 band with no direction, a sharp RISE from 07:44 to 08:44 (64.0 -> 70.3), then a partial REVERSAL back to 68.0 where it has since held flat.\n\n**What survives, stated at the confidence the data supports.** There is a real net drift across the night — 57.3 to 68.0 is not noise. But the rate is not constant, the peak has already retreated, and no responsible deadline can be read off this. The urgency I implied was manufactured by my own arithmetic.\n\n**What the reversal adds, which is the genuinely useful part.** Something on this host RECLAIMS. That is new information and it narrows the search: the producer has a lifecycle — allocate then release — rather than being a monotonic leak. Static orphaned content cannot explain a signal that falls.\n\n**A hypothesis this suggests, explicitly not a finding.** The 07:44-08:44 rise coincides with review-bot activity: changes 2669 and 2670 were LLM-reviewed in that window (LLM-Review +1 on 2669 at 08:36:55 UTC), and the fall begins immediately after. Closed bug `1ef8` records that the review-bot's clone path writes onto ROOT when its scratch volume is declared but unmounted — and that volume IS currently unmounted here (`gate_scratch_mounted` reads 0 on every sample; open ticket `dcc3`). Per-review clones landing on root would produce exactly a rise-during-reviews, fall-after signature. The discriminating test is whether clone directories under root carry mtimes inside the rise window; I have handed that to the diagnosis agent rather than concluding it.\n\nThis is the third measurement of mine tonight to need correcting, and the three have different causes worth separating: the first was a wrong namespace and a misread timezone offset, the second was citing a metric with a filed defect, and this one was over-fitting a rate to an oscillating series. Only the first was a slip. The other two were me stating more than the evidence carried."},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1788600668631634792,"uuid":"57923b31-0fd4-4f06-a5e1-5cfd9d4158b7"}